// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// lint_waivers.vlt — Verilator warning waivers (SPEC 12.1).
//
// SPEC 12.1: "Warnings may be waived only through a checked-in waiver file
// containing a written justification."
//
// Rules for this file
// -------------------
//  1. Every waiver is as narrow as the warning allows: prefer
//     `-file` + `-lines` + `-match` over a bare rule name, and never waive a
//     rule design-wide.
//  2. Every waiver carries a justification comment stating *why* the warning is
//     wrong or accepted here, and what would make it removable.
//  3. A waiver that outlives its cause is a defect. When the owning issue lands,
//     delete the waiver and re-run `make lint`.
//  4. Fixing the RTL always beats adding a waiver. WIDTH, LATCH, CASEINCOMPLETE
//     and BLKSEQ waivers are not acceptable: they hide real bugs.
//
// `make lint` runs `verilator --lint-only --Wall` *without* `--Wno-fatal`, so
// any warning not waived here fails the build with a non-zero exit status.
//
// Current count: 2 waivers, both the same shape — a generated package that
// exports a complete definition, of which the current phase consumes part. Keep
// it that way.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

`verilator_config

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// UNUSEDPARAM in the generated configuration package.
//
// Why it fires: config_pkg.sv is generated from config/<name>.json and exports
// the complete elaboration parameter set (SPEC 11 sized parameters plus SPEC 3
// invariants) as one source of truth shared by the RTL, the C++ harness and,
// later, the Quartus flow. At Phase 0 the only RTL in the design is the
// provisional stream loopback, so most of those parameters have no consumer yet.
//
// Why it is not a defect: the alternative is to emit only the parameters the
// current phase happens to use, which would make the package's content depend on
// build order and would silently drop a parameter whenever RTL is removed. The
// unused parameters are consumed as the pipeline is built out: FFT_SIZE and
// PFB_TAPS by issues #10-#11, N_BEAMS by #12, HISTORY_FRAMES by #15 (consumed:
// sim/verilator/tops/history_top.sv checks its DUT 0 against it at time zero),
// PACKET_W and N_VIRTUAL_CHANS by #18. The waiver stays until the last of them
// lands; it is the FILE that is waived, not any one parameter.
//
// Scope: the generated package file only, and only this message text. Verified
// narrow — with this waiver in place, adding an unused parameter to
// rtl/common/stream_loopback.sv still fails `make lint`. Any other file
// reporting UNUSEDPARAM is a real dead parameter and must be fixed, not waived.
//
// Note on syntax: Verilator 5.020 ignores `-file` on its own for this rule (the
// warning is attributed to the elaborated instance rather than the source file),
// so the waiver needs `-file` *and* `-match`. `lint_off -module` is not accepted.
//
// Removable when: the full-scale elaboration (issue #20) consumes every
// parameter. Delete this waiver then and re-run `make lint`.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
lint_off -rule UNUSEDPARAM -file "*config_pkg.sv" -match "Parameter is not used*"

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// UNUSEDPARAM in the generated register-map package (issue #7).
//
// Why it fires: rtl/control/generated/regmap_pkg.sv is generated by
// scripts/gen_regmap.py from control/regmap.json and exports the WHOLE register
// map — every block window, every register index and address, and every field's
// LSB, width and mask. The RTL consumes the tables and the indices it needs to
// build the fabric and the blocks; the per-field slice constants exist for the
// consumers that arrive with the blocks they belong to (enables in #10-#15,
// counters in #8, coefficients in #10/#12), and for a reader who wants the map
// and the RTL to agree in one file. The C++ half of the same map
// (model/cpp/regmap/regmap.hpp) uses them today; SystemVerilog does not yet.
//
// Why it is not a defect: the alternative is to emit only the constants some
// module currently references, which would make a generated package's content
// depend on which RTL happens to exist, silently drop a field whenever a
// consumer is deleted, and break the property that the artefact is a pure
// function of the source of truth — the property `make regmap-check` relies on.
// It is the same trade, for the same reason, as the config_pkg waiver above.
//
// Scope: the generated package file only, and only this message text. Verified
// narrow — with this waiver in place, an unused parameter in any hand-written
// file under rtl/control/ still fails `make lint`.
//
// Removable when: every register group in control/regmap.json is implemented and
// wired (issues #8, #10-#15, #19). Delete this waiver then and re-run `make lint`.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
lint_off -rule UNUSEDPARAM -file "*regmap_pkg.sv" -match "Parameter is not used*"

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// UNUSEDSIGNAL in the medium pipeline top (issue #17).
//
// Why it fires: rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv (module `benchmark_pipeline_top`)
// instantiates seven fully-featured verification blocks (PFB, FFT, history,
// alignment, beamformer, power/covariance, CFAR), each of which exports rich
// telemetry, observation and per-block status ports. The Phase 3 integration
// consumes the subset that reaches the pipeline's top-level status bus and
// binds every remaining port to a named `_dummy` sink so PINMISSING and
// PINCONNECTEMPTY are not silenced. Verilator's UNUSEDSIGNAL rule then
// reports every one of those named sinks; the sinks exist precisely to
// document which outputs the pipeline deliberately does not export.
//
// Why it is not a defect: the alternative is to leave each unused port
// unconnected, which is exactly the PINCONNECTEMPTY warning the rest of the
// repo works to avoid (rtl/cdc/stream_cdc.sv makes this point explicitly).
// A named sink is the documented convention here for exports the top
// intentionally drops. Same shape as align_top.sv and history_top.sv.
//
// Scope: only rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv, only UNUSEDSIGNAL for signals
// whose name ends in `_dummy`. Any other unused signal in that file (or any
// other UNUSEDSIGNAL rule outside it) still fails `make lint`.
//
// Removable when: the register plane (issue #19) or the packet fabric
// integration (issue #18/#19) consumes each per-block telemetry group. The
// waiver is deliberately narrow so its removal is a targeted operation.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv" -match "*_dummy*"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*rtl/top/benchmark_pipeline_ctrl.sv" -match "*'head_sof'*"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv" -match "*'cfar_out_fields'*"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv" -match "*'bf_out_fields'*"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv" -match "*'pfb_frame_v'*"
lint_off -rule UNUSEDSIGNAL -file "*rtl/top/benchmark_sim_top.sv" -match "*'rf'*"
